----
{#motivation slip enter="~duration:0.5"}
----
> # Motivation

{#motivation-part-1}
> - If someone wants to play a game developed for retro game systems like [NES](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Entertainment_System), [Game Boy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_Boy), [3DS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_3DS), [Wii](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii), etc., they'd install an emulator like [Dolphin](https://dolphin-emu.org), [mGBA](https://mgba.io), [Panda3DS](https://panda3ds.com), etc., load the ROM file into it, and play via it.
>
> {style="display: flex; gap: 40px; align-items: center; justify-content: center;"}
> ![](./assets/nes.png){width=500px}
> ![](./assets/gameboy.png){width=300px}
> ![](./assets/3ds.png){width=420px}
> ![](./assets/wii.png){width=450px}

{pause=motivation-part-1 up="~duration:0.5 motivation-part-1"}

{#motivation-part-2}
> - All the aforementioned emulator tools use techniques like simple interpreted based emulation or dynamic compilation (JIT-based).
> - Static recompilation is another approach, where the ROM file is recompiled ahead-of-time into a native executable for a different system.
> - It is particularly visible in N64 ecosystem due to tools like [N64Recomp](https://github.com/N64Recomp/N64Recomp). It is being used in a project named [Zelda64Recomp](https://github.com/Zelda64Recomp/Zelda64Recomp) which is focused on statically recompiling [Majora's Mask](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda%3A_Majora%27s_Mask).

{pause=motivation-part-2 up="~duration:0.5 motivation-part-2"}

{#motivation-part-3}
> - There are other tools like [Remill](https://github.com/lifting-bits/remill), which is developed by [Trail of Bits](https://trailofbits.com), which uses the idea of lifting binaries to LLVM IR for code portability between different systems.

{pause=motivation-part-3 up="~duration:0.5 motivation-part-3"}

{pause focus="~duration:0.5 motivation"}

----
{#idea slip enter="~duration:0.5"}
----
> # Idea

{#idea-part-1}
> - After looking at these static recompilation tools and [Andrew Kelley's blog post](https://andrewkelley.me/post/jamulator.html), we came up with the idea of gbrc.
> - gbrc uses LLVM’s infrastructure to compile a `.gb` file into LLVM IR, then uses any LLVM-compatible compiler to generate a native binary for that specific system.
> - To produce the native binary for a new target platform, the requirements are: an LLVM backend for that platform and implementing 7 frontend hooks as mentioned in our runtime library for developing the frontend.

{pause=idea-part-1 up="~duration:0.5 idea-part-1"}

{#idea-part-2}
> - The runtime library is also extremely minimal, making it suitable for running on embedded systems as well, such as microcontrollers like the RP2040, which uses an ARM Cortex-M0, and LLVM has an in-tree ARM target.

{pause=idea-part-2 up="~duration:0.5 idea-part-2"}

{pause focus="~duration:0.5 idea"}

